  {"type": "split", "h2": "Why hard water in parts of Ontario matters for your tankless", "image": "Hot water running at a bathroom sink", "image_src": "/assets/img/brands/rinnai-hot-water-sink.webp", "image_fit": "cover", "image_pos": "50% 50%", "image_credit": "Image: Rinnai", "html": "<p>Much of Southern Ontario sits on limestone, and the groundwater that comes with it is hard. Kitchener-Waterloo, Barrie, Newmarket and well-water homes across York, Durham and Simcoe all see it. Even Lake Ontario municipal water carries enough minerals to matter over time.</p><p>Inside a tankless unit, that water passes through a narrow heat exchanger at high temperature, which is exactly where scale likes to form. A layer of limescale insulates the exchanger, so the burner runs longer, the outlet temperature drops, flow slows, and the unit throws codes. Left long enough it cracks the exchanger, the most expensive part in the unit. Manufacturers know this, which is why the warranty expects an annual flush. On well water we also talk about a softener or a scale inhibitor ahead of the heater.</p>"},
